; Parchment frame layout: start position and 11 rows of 24 codes
;
; Read by #R$9C96 (#R$9CA5 draws a row, #R$9CB7 one code). The first word is the position of the frame: low byte =
; character row (5), high byte = column (4). Then 11 rows of 24 cells follow, each cell described by a code: code 1 is
; followed by a count n and means n blank cells (only the attribute is set, the bitmap is left as it is); any other
; code k draws frame tile k-2 from #R$717F. The first row is the top border, the next 9 rows are a left tile, a run of
; 22 blank cells (code 1, $16) and a right tile, the last row is the bottom border. A 0 where a row would start ends
; the layout.
; .
; The window therefore covers 24 x 11 character cells, columns 4-27 and rows 5-15; the text of a parchment is printed
; over its blank cells.
@label=ParchmentLayout
b$71DF DEFW $0405
 $71E1 DEFB $02,$03,$04,$03,$03,$04,$03,$03,$03,$03,$03,$03,$03,$03,$04,$03
 $71F1 DEFB $03,$03,$03,$04,$03,$04,$03,$05
 $71F9 DEFB $06,$01,$16,$09
 $71FD DEFB $08,$01,$16,$09
 $7201 DEFB $06,$01,$16,$07
 $7205 DEFB $08,$01,$16,$07
 $7209 DEFB $08,$01,$16,$07
 $720D DEFB $08,$01,$16,$07
 $7211 DEFB $08,$01,$16,$09
